{"summary":"Accepted the hatch-diagnostics improvement and the missing explicit GH suppression test. Rejected the condition-floor proposal as a tuning preference rather than a demonstrated defect, and rejected both alkalinity-related amendments because their stated premises do not match the current worktree.","votes":[{"amendment_id":"fr-7-ae2bf47c","decision":"accept","rationale":"ShrimpHatched currently always emits only RoutineAction even when hatch_rate is materially suppressed. Because hatching uses deterministic rounding with carry, a small cohort can fully hatch without any accompanying EggFailure event, so users lose visibility into the limiting factor. Reusing the existing EggFailure cause tagging for sub-threshold successful hatches is low-risk and improves diagnosability."},{"amendment_id":"fr-7-8e8338a4","decision":"reject","rationale":"The condition factor is the shrimp's already-smoothed internal body-state signal, not an external stress curve like density, TAN, or nitrite. Letting condition_index reach 0.0 and drive readiness to 0.0 is coherent for catastrophic condition, while adding a floor would reintroduce breeding pressure even at zero condition. The amendment identifies a possible tuning choice, but it does not demonstrate a correctness or safety bug."},{"amendment_id":"fr-7-5d1c7cc7","decision":"accept","rationale":"The cited test directly asserts TAN and NO2 suppression against a clean baseline, but it does not do the same for very low GH. Existing GH coverage is indirect and does not clearly satisfy the acceptance criterion that low GH independently suppress breeding. A dedicated low-GH readiness assertion would close a real regression gap."},{"amendment_id":"fr-7-e6495363","decision":"reject","rationale":"The premise is not true in the current worktree: crates/tank_core/tests/nitrification_alkalinity.rs is already tracked by git, and git status for that path is clean. Since the file is already under version control, this is not a real patch-integrity defect."},{"amendment_id":"fr-7-43506246","decision":"reject","rationale":"The current budget_helpers Element surface is explicitly limited to the mg-based nitrogen/carbon/oxygen ledger, and its docs state that alkalinity-in-meq diagnostics remain on BudgetMetric instead. There is no Element::Alkalinity path at the cited lines, so those hard-coded mg labels are not currently producing scientifically wrong alkalinity assertion output. This amendment does not match an actual defect in the present code."}]}